WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:01.970
- This episode of LegalEagle was

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.570
made possible by Skillshare.

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.100
Learn to think like a lawyer for free

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.900
for two month by clicking
the link in the description.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.110
Okay, I'll admit it,

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:11.440
I was pleasantly surprised
by "The Witcher".

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.430
Interesting characters,
compelling narratives

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.920
and new takes on an old genre.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.920
But then I got to this part.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:19.410
- Lioness of Cintra!

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.783
I come to claim what is rightfully mine.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.421
Pavetta, by the law of surprise.

00:00:25.421 --> 00:00:26.350
(gasps)

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:27.183
- Oh.

00:00:27.183 --> 00:00:29.360
Cool, the law of surprise.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:30.820
That thing.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.720
Wait, the what now?

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.840
And then a number of thoughts
went through my head.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.670
Okay, Devin, you went to law school.

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:38.890
This is clearly a law, this is a thing.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:42.430
You know this, think Devin, law school.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:46.690
Contracts, offer,
acceptance, law of surprise?

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:47.523
Think!

00:00:47.523 --> 00:00:50.210
You don't want Thomas Frank
to be make fun of you again.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:54.130
Wait, there's no such thing
as the law of surprise!

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:54.963
Is there?

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.023
(bleep)

00:00:58.023 --> 00:01:00.163
It was an emotional couple of moments.

00:01:01.293 --> 00:01:03.850
(joyful music)

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.340
Hey LegalEagles, it's time
to think like a lawyer,

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.370
because I'm trying to
figure out "The Witcher",

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:09.690
and if I have to do it then,

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:11.507
by God, I'm taking you with me.

00:01:11.507 --> 00:01:14.930
"The Witcher" is the newest
entree into the fantasy genre,

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:16.980
an eight-episode series on Netflix

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.290
for those who are still going

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:19.700
through "Game of Thrones" withdrawal.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.880
The show is based on a set
of short stories published

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.210
by Polish author, Andrzej Sapkowski,

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.070
and also gave rise to the

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.270
famous "Witcher" videogames as well.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:31.040
The main plot centers around
a few main characters.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.380
The central protagonist, Geralt of Rivia,

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:34.650
the eponymous witcher.

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.480
The mage, Yennefer of Vengerberg

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:37.840
and Princess Cirilla.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.110
Their lives are intertwined

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:41.280
for a variety of reasons,
most of which are tied

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.690
to the vague concept of destiny.

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:47.310
- Destiny has determined
the surprise be Pavetta.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.190
- If you're unfamiliar with the books,

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:51.420
then the show itself can
be a little bit confusing,

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.440
based on the different roles,

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.750
between the witcher, elves, mages,

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.000
and there's a lot to
absorb and understand.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.990
But, possibly none are more
confusing and controversial

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:03.610
than "The Witcher"'s law of surprise.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.470
The law of surprise is introduced

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:06.980
in episode four of "The Witcher",

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:08.593
when during a soiree...

00:02:08.593 --> 00:02:12.490
Also, they refer to this
party as a soiree many times

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:16.030
and this french neologism
seems way out of place.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.250
At any rate, during this
soiree, the point is

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:22.720
to find a suitor for Queen
Calanthe's daughter, Pavetta.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.210
And during the course
of these suitors trying

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:29.440
to woo Pavetta, a helmeted
man, Duny, comes in claiming

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:30.520
that the queen's daughter is

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.800
actually owed to him, due to his right

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.740
under the law of surprise.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.473
- I am Lord Urcheon of Erlenwald

00:02:38.407 --> 00:02:41.030
and I have come to claim your
daughter's hand in marriage.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.470
- He claims to have earned
this right by saving the life

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:46.130
of the queen's late husband, King Roegner.

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:48.960
- My whole life, a living
misery, until the day

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.270
that I saved your husband, King Roegner,

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:52.750
from a certain death.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.960
By tradition, I chose the
law of surprise as payment.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.230
- Duny, or the knight of
Urcheon, demands his payment

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.801
in the form of Pavetta's hand in marriage.

00:03:02.801 --> 00:03:03.634
- Duny!

00:03:05.836 --> 00:03:06.880
(Duny gasps)

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.570
- But don't worry, this isn't rapey,

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.961
because they conveniently already met

00:03:10.961 --> 00:03:15.380
and fell in love off-screen,
in Canada probably.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:17.520
So, Pavetta is what lawyers would call

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.380
his chattel property.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:24.230
- Until destiny intervened
and our hearts collided.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:27.170
- But also, girl power.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:28.003
I guess.

00:03:28.003 --> 00:03:31.130
So, despite the Me Too issues
with the law of surprise,

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:34.040
on it's face, the law is
relatively straightforward.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:35.390
For payment of a great deed,

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:38.270
usually saving someone's
life, one can claim something

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:40.190
which the debtor does not yet possess

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:42.230
at the moment the law is claimed.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:44.980
It's a surprise for the
giver and the receiver.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:46.060
The tricky part is,

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.850
there appears to be two possible outcomes.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:50.560
For the one invoking the law of surprise,

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.040
they will receive either the first thing

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.780
that comes to greet the indebted

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.640
when they return home after
their life is being saved

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.340
or what you find at home
and you don't expect.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:02.350
Geralt, is apparently super cool

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:05.500
with this arranged marriage
slash indentured servitude

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.440
and when given the chance to repay Duny,

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.110
for saving his life, he
claims the same right

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:13.770
regarding Pavetta, and thus
the circle of slavery continues

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:17.571
with Pavetta's child, Ciri,
being contracted to Geralt.

00:04:17.571 --> 00:04:20.820
- People linked by destiny
will always find each other.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:22.028
- Feminism.

00:04:22.028 --> 00:04:23.150
(Calanthe groans)
(heavy thud)

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:25.580
So, those are the terms
of the law of surprise,

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:27.240
but is it legal?

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.210
Let's take a look at
the legal enforceability

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:30.043
of the law of surprise

00:04:30.043 --> 00:04:32.090
as if it were applied today in the,

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.900
usually non fantasy, world the we live in.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:36.090
Now, right of the bat,

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:38.080
we can see that there's a huge problem

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.120
when dealing with any law

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:41.310
that allows one to acquire another person

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.020
as a designated method of payment.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:45.817
As you probably know, the
Thirteenth Amendment states,

00:04:45.817 --> 00:04:48.137
"Neither slavery nor
involuntary servitude,

00:04:48.137 --> 00:04:49.187
"except as punishment

00:04:49.187 --> 00:04:51.587
"for crime whereof the
party shall have been

00:04:51.587 --> 00:04:54.007
"duly convicted, shall exist
within the United States,

00:04:54.007 --> 00:04:56.070
"or any subject to their jurisdiction."

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.720
So, considering in both
instances that we see on screen

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:00.810
in "The Witcher", the law
of surprise is invoked

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.252
to claim the daughter
of one who was saved,

00:05:03.252 --> 00:05:05.930
it appears that this would
clearly be a violation

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:09.290
of the Thirteenth Amendment
against involuntary servitude.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:11.700
Now, sure, Pavetta
seemed perfectly content

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:13.100
with being given over to Duny,

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.800
since they had already fallen in love...

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:16.830
conveniently.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.410
But, I've got to imagine that
that's more of the exception,

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:20.610
rather than the rule.

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:22.560
Clearly, the witcher has no problem

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:24.423
with involuntary servitude.

00:05:24.423 --> 00:05:26.250
- A promise made must be honored.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.510
- But Bill of Rights, shmill of rights!

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:30.420
Let's think beyond the Constitution.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.410
What if we assume that the law of surprise

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.370
resulted in the transfer
of something other

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:37.837
than humans, maybe a new puppy.

00:05:37.837 --> 00:05:39.830
"A pup", as Eist refers to it.

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:41.760
Or a new car that your significant other

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.430
brought for you while you were away.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:46.330
Would the law of surprise be legal then?

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:48.240
Well, to answer this
question it would require

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.430
a foray into the complicated
world of contract law.

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:53.838
And just as the delightful Jaskier says...

00:05:53.838 --> 00:05:56.320
- Respect doesn't make history.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.450
- Well, contract law probably doesn't make

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:01.290
for great fantasy fiction,
but let's take a look

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.480
and see how we might make a workable

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:05.530
law of surprise in real life.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:07.420
Now, by way of background, contract law,

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:09.740
unlike some other areas
of law, like criminal law,

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:11.620
is not really codified into a bunch of

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.410
neatly packaged statutes.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:16.950
Contract law is primarily
developed from the common law.

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:17.963
- 'Tis no swindle.

00:06:18.831 --> 00:06:20.920
Asking for payment with
the law of surprise

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:22.970
is as old as mankind itself.

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.250
- Generally contract law is a blend of

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:27.550
state case law that's developed by judges,

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:30.600
and statutes that are
passed by state legislators.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:31.510
So, first things first,

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:33.430
how is a contract formed?

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:35.380
Well, in order for
there for be a contract,

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:37.060
there's certain requirements or elements

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.270
that have to be met, and while it varies

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:40.340
a little bit from state to state,

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.220
generally those are: an offer,

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:44.240
an acceptance of that offer,

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:46.060
consideration between the parties,

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:47.950
mutuality of obligation,

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:49.870
competency and capacity,

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:51.390
and in certain circumstances,

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:54.400
though certainly not all
or even most circumstances,

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.230
a written instrument, a written contract.

00:06:57.230 --> 00:06:58.590
So, let's begin with the offer.

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:00.310
An offer, for purposes of contract law,

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:02.610
is a promise to do or refrain from doing

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:04.250
something specific in the future.

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:06.520
An offer can be a simple
as, party A offers

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:09.340
to mow party B's lawn next Tuesday.

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:10.550
So, in the case of "The Witcher",

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:12.370
right off the bat, it's hard to see how

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:13.970
the law of surprise even meets the first

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:15.600
element of contract formation,

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:18.340
in that it doesn't contain a valid offer.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:21.150
It's more like a gratuitous gift.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:24.240
Essentially, half of the
exchange is already accomplished.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:26.500
For example, King Roegner's life has

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:27.850
already been saved by Duny,

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:29.630
so that there's no reason for a

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:32.160
contract to be formed
in order for payment,

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.610
because nothing was
offered in the first place.

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:36.230
Now, on the other hand,
just because the parties

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:38.120
don't expressly exchange words,

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:40.360
it's still possible that based
on their actions something,

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.960
known as an implied-in-fact
contract, has occurred.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.210
An implied-in-fact
contract is an obligation

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:48.770
arising from a mutual agreement
and intent to promise,

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:50.860
where the agreement and the
promise haven't actually

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:52.760
been expressed in words.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:54.150
Now, regardless of the means of

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:56.140
expressing the contract,
an implied-in-fact contract

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:58.100
still requires an unambiguous offer,

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:00.310
acceptance and mutual intent to be bound,

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:01.470
and of course, consideration.

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:02.880
But, here we run into the same problems.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:03.980
The deed has already been done,

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:06.730
the life has been saved
under circumstances

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:08.990
that don't give rise to a contract

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:12.100
or an implied contract, so we
still have the same problems.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:13.650
But for the sake of
argument, let's consider

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:16.100
the fact that we're calling
this the law of surprise.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:18.090
So, in this fictional scenario,

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:19.750
the law of surprise was passed by

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:22.000
a governing body and
codified into a statute,

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.150
and thus no offer or
acceptance is required,

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:28.610
merely a life being saved
requires payment in return.

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:29.510
So, for the sake of argument,

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:31.560
let's assume that the, I don't know,

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:33.954
legislator or parliament of Cintra,

00:08:33.954 --> 00:08:35.947
has passed this law of surprise

00:08:35.947 --> 00:08:38.530
and it doesn't require offer or acceptance

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:41.120
in terms of these circumstances,

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:42.340
where you save a life.

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:44.230
Let's move on to the third element

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:46.510
that's required for a
contract to be formed,

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:47.590
and that's consideration.

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:50.013
And consideration is
generally described as

00:08:50.013 --> 00:08:53.240
promising something of value in exchange

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:56.560
for a specified action or
non-action of another person.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:58.520
Essentially, value for value.

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:02.060
So, if you agree to
give a gift to someone,

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:03.870
that's not a contract, because you have

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:06.070
gratuitously offered something to them

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:09.480
and received no consideration
back for your promise.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:12.350
And if that's the case,
that might actually work.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:14.580
You obviously have performed
something of great value,

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:15.717
you saved someone's life,

00:09:15.717 --> 00:09:17.750
and that is extremely valuable

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:19.290
to the person whose life was saved,

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:22.160
and in return you're being
promised this other thing.

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:24.030
You don't know what it is,

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:26.237
but it might be something of great value,

00:09:26.237 --> 00:09:28.810
and the law allows for you to contract for

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:33.180
something as valueless as a,

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:34.490
they call it a peppercorn.

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:36.470
Yes, it's a very, very low value,

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:38.730
but it's value nonetheless and you are

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:41.850
allowed to contract based
on that mutual exchange.

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:44.100
So, this law of surpise
actually kinda works,

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:45.450
in terms of consideration,

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:48.630
but that raises another problem,

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:52.150
which is that often a
defense to a contract

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.350
is vagueness or ambiguity,

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:56.950
and here's where things
really start to get dicey.

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:59.640
Courts require what's called
a meeting of the minds,

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:00.960
to form a contract.

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.120
That means that the parties agree to what

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:04.710
the terms of the contract are,

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:06.360
and if we look back at the law of surprise

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.740
we see that the thing
that is being promised is

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:10.540
either the first thing that comes to greet

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:11.860
the debtor when they return home

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:13.340
after their life has been saved,

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:16.700
or what they find at
home and didn't expect.

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:20.150
So, in a word, that sounds ambiguous.

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:23.590
Now, on the other hand, every
contract involves some risk.

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:25.440
Let's say you formed a
contract with a farmer,

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:27.894
to buy their entire crop for the year.

00:10:27.894 --> 00:10:29.980
You might get a huge bumper crop,

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:31.750
or you might get nothing, because the crop

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:33.390
was wiped out because of a drought.

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:35.310
So, there's always an inherent risk

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:37.981
and some ambiguity in
terms of performance,

00:10:37.981 --> 00:10:41.290
but this also seems like you have no idea

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:43.740
what you're contracting
for in the first place.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.760
On top of that, there's overall ambiguity

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:48.830
as to how this contract would be enforced.

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:51.670
Even the specific words
used in the law of surprise,

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:54.910
like greet, or the time frame that

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:56.250
we're talking about here could be open

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:57.940
to multiple interpretations.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:10:59.390
What does it mean to be greeted with

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:00.920
something when you get home?

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:02.350
What if the first thing that happens

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:04.530
is your dog runs out and
licks you in the face?

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:07.220
What if your spouse is angry with you

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:08.090
and starts yelling at you?

00:11:08.090 --> 00:11:09.560
What if there're multiple things

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:11.680
that are new that you didn't
expect when you got home?

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:12.600
What if there're twins?

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:13.510
Do you owe both of them?

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:14.460
Or just one?

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:17.140
The options and
interpretations are endless.

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:18.360
I feel like, at the very least,

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:20.820
everyone in this fictional
world of "The Witcher"

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:23.150
needs a good lawyer to talk about all

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:24.630
of these different eventualities.

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:27.720
And then, of course, we run
into huge performance issues.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:30.210
In contract law,
generally your damages are

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:32.050
measured in money.

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:33.470
Sometimes you're entitled to what's

00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:34.770
called specific performance,

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:36.180
or in other words compelling

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:38.210
one party to turn over the other.

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:40.010
For example, that happens
a lot in real estate,

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:42.190
where a piece of real estate is considered

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:45.420
so unique that money
damages are not enough

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:49.550
to compensate someone for
the loss of that property,

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:51.080
that was supposed to be turned over.

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:53.220
And if we are talking about money damages

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.240
for a person that was promised

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:56.700
and doesn't want to go through with

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:58.140
the indentured servitude,

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:01.450
how are we supposed to
value that person's life?

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:03.820
In daylights and sunsets?

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:06.150
In midnights or cups of coffee?

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:09.210
Basically impossible to
put a value on that person.

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:11.850
And there's at least one
other contract offense,

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:14.810
that would probably bar a
law of surprise contract

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:15.850
from being formed,

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:17.670
which is called the statute of frauds.

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:20.170
Now, I mentioned earlier
that most contracts

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:21.800
actually don't need to be written.

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:23.330
That is a common misconception.

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:25.200
You can have an oral contact,

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:27.420
as long as you meet the other requirements

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:30.100
for contract formation, but the law

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:32.610
requires that certain contracts do,

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:34.340
in fact, be written down.

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:36.430
The idea is that certain contracts

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:38.920
are so important, that to prevent fraud,

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:41.400
we need to require a written document.

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:42.970
Now, the types of contracts to which

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:45.400
this defense applies, are contracts often

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:47.550
that involve the sale or transfer of land,

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:50.080
contracts that cannot be
completed within one year,

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:53.040
and those made in
connection with marriage.

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:55.440
And two of those scenarios could be argued

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:57.620
by a defendant in challenging
the enforceability

00:12:57.620 --> 00:12:58.960
of the law of surprise.

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:00.820
Certainly, the example of Duny and Pavetta

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:03.290
highlights both of these contract types.

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:05.010
Duny didn't claim the law of surprise

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:07.230
for Pavetta until 15 years after

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:09.360
the contract was made.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:12.250
- I knew no woman would
ever accept it like this,

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:13.373
and so I waited.

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:16.800
I waited until the twelfth bell,

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:18.350
when the curse breaks.

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:20.210
- And on top of that,
Duny is claiming that

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:22.800
his contract entitles
him to marry Pavetta,

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:24.120
based on the law of surprise,

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:26.010
and thus, we have a legitimate defense,

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:27.970
that under the statute of frauds,

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:30.900
that this contract was
required to be in writing,

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:32.140
and it wasn't.

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:33.740
Now, is there any way that we could

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:36.060
revise this law of surprise,

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:39.350
in order to make a
legally binding contract?

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:40.900
Well, I think so, but there are a

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:42.270
couple of things that
we'd need to address.

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:44.160
First, we have the ambiguity problem,

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:45.737
so we need to get rid of this,

00:13:45.737 --> 00:13:47.677
"You're entitled to whatever it is that

00:13:47.677 --> 00:13:49.970
"the debtor finds upon returning home."

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:53.260
Instead of saying it's whatever they find,

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:55.367
at the time of contract formation you say,

00:13:55.367 --> 00:14:00.347
"I want, in return for saving your life,

00:14:00.347 --> 00:14:01.937
"this specific thing.

00:14:01.937 --> 00:14:02.887
"Your new car.

00:14:02.887 --> 00:14:04.047
"Your horse.

00:14:04.047 --> 00:14:05.610
"Your newborn pup."

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:08.080
By specifying the things
that you are entitled to,

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:10.170
you remove a lot of the ambiguity.

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:13.090
On top of that, in order
to solve the problem

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:16.270
of offer and acceptance, instead of

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:18.550
claiming the law of
surprise after you save

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:21.480
someone's life, you might want to say

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:22.447
before you save their life,

00:14:22.447 --> 00:14:24.497
"Yes, I will in fact save your life,

00:14:24.497 --> 00:14:27.580
"but only if you promise me, X, Y or Z."

00:14:27.580 --> 00:14:29.080
By proactively doing it,

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:31.000
in a sort of underhanded way,

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:33.600
they are promising to give you that thing,

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:36.020
in return for your consideration of

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:38.960
saving their life, obviously
something of great value.

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:41.670
And, of course, leave the women out of it.

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:43.681
You can't contract for people.

00:14:43.681 --> 00:14:46.510
(gentle piano music)

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:49.020
Now, of course, this kind
of takes the surprise

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:50.240
out of the law of surprise,

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:51.980
but, I mean, I don't know
what you want from me.

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:53.807
No one ever spoke to their
lawyer, and was like,

00:14:53.807 --> 00:14:55.027
"I love talking to my lawyer.

00:14:55.027 --> 00:14:56.630
"They let me do whatever I want."

00:14:56.630 --> 00:14:58.140
So yeah, the law of surprise,

00:14:58.140 --> 00:14:59.460
as it's portrayed in "The Witcher",

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:02.090
has more legal holes in
it than Swiss cheese.

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:03.980
Certainly, if someone
brought a breach of contract

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:05.980
case based on the law of surprise,

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:08.610
the court would strike down that contract

00:15:08.610 --> 00:15:11.040
as invalid on any number of grounds,

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:13.400
but based on some minor adjustments,

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:16.780
the law of surprise might
give rise to a valid contract.

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:18.450
But if we've learnt
anything from "The Witcher",

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:20.240
it's that we should appreciate those who

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:23.810
roam the land looking to
right wrongs and do justice,

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:26.490
and I think you know
who I'm talking about.

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:28.150
So, if you could...

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:30.745
Please...

00:15:30.745 --> 00:15:33.946
♪ Toss a coin to your lawyer ♪

00:15:33.946 --> 00:15:36.358
